   :reviewer-should-check "(1) WHERE THE DECLARATION LIVES. I put :threshold and :observable-kind on the per-call selector rather than on the criterion's :spec, so c-distribution is untouched. That is the cheap form for a comparison and it is NOT the shipping form: if an arm needing a declaration wins, the declaration belongs in the :spec and c-distribution has to carry it, which will move the map that c-distribution-is-the-pinned-constructor compares. If you think that move should have happened now, say so. (2) THE REFUSAL GRANULARITY. Under an arm, one criterion the arm declines to read makes the WHOLE risk absent (:unread-outcome, with the refusals listed), following the discipline :unreadable-observable already applies. The alternative -- score the criteria the arm accepts and renormalise the weights over them -- would give :typed-binary-only a number on some fields instead of none, and would change what the sweep in section 2 says about coverage. I chose refusal because a partial sum is a different mission's risk wearing this one's name, but it IS a choice and it shapes the 4/14 and 0/14 counts. (3) THE MIRROR. Clause (b) numbers come from readback-under in the script, not from war_machine's readback, because the shipped one takes no arm. Section 3 is the control; check that it compares enough fields (:mission :criteria-source-sha256 :criterion-count :measurable-count :mission-action-count :non-mission-action-count :status :reason, plus the per-action risk value) and not merely enough to pass. (4) :declared-binarization WITH NO THRESHOLD still reads an exactly-binary VALUE (0.0 or 1.0) rather than refusing. That is why it agrees with :numeric-equality on the four zero channels and scores 4/14 instead of 0/14. The stricter reading -- refuse unless a threshold is declared, whatever the value -- would make it identical to :typed-binary-only on this corpus and arguably clearer. I took the looser one because the row's own wording is 'a Bernoulli spec on a CONTINUOUS