   :statement "BRANCHES BUILT AND RUN for the Bernoulli outcome semantics (U12's defect, verified by claude-1 at preferences.clj:242; Joe's 2026-09-01 rule: a choice the theory does not settle gets branches built and run, not an advance ruling): log-preference's Bernoulli branch selects the non-target outcome with (= 0 x), false for double 0.0, so the unsatisfied pole is unreachable from R2's all-doubles observation vector, and nil scores as SATISFIED. Scope: only U11's mission_c.clj calls this path; C_int unaffected. Build the three candidate semantics behind a declared per-call selector (no default change): (1) == numeric equality; (2) declared binarization -- a Bernoulli spec on a continuous observable must carry a threshold, else refuse; (3) typed refusal on spec/observable mismatch, == for genuinely binary observables. Under ALL arms nil is a typed absence, never satisfied (C130-entailed, not a branch). Run all three over the U12 corpus and the 14-channel sweep; report where they agree, where they differ, and what each does to (b)-discrimination and (c)-satisfied-zero. mission_c_test.clj:197-205 (pins the long-0 vocabulary the live path cannot speak) gains a double-0.0 and nil case per arm."
