   :evidence "futon2 5cadd3c (war_machine.clj + war_machine_test.clj -- SEE THE COMMIT-SWEEP NOTE AT THE END, the message on that sha is not this row's) + this commit (registry entry, replay script, artifacts, ledger). THE DESIGN, HALF (a). The seam, restated with pointers: load-active-mission reads the durable clock at the TOP of the tick (war_machine.clj:1549) and record-selection-clock! writes the new edge AFTER the trace is persisted (war_machine.clj:1986) inside a future whose failure is printed to stderr and dropped. U21 named the lag; what it left open is that a durable edge one tick stale because the write LANDED and one stale because the write NEVER LANDED are indistinguishable from inside a tick. EIGHT CASES ENUMERATED, as a closed vocabulary at war_machine.clj:1631-1667: :clock-not-read, :durable-unreadable, :no-durable-edge, :no-selection-this-tick, :agrees, :lagged-write-landed, :lagged-write-missing, :lag-unattributable. THE OPTION CHOSEN, with the other two rejected in the registry entry: `recorded divergence that a later tick clears`. Grounds: the same-tick witnessed edge changes what a clock edge MEANS and moves the write failure earlier rather than removing it (and the row's statement puts edge-minting out of scope); the typed pending state cannot be resolved by the tick that writes it, so it is this option with an extra field. THE DISCRIMINATOR IS A WITNESS, NOT AN INFERENCE: the clock edge already carries witness.source, the identity of the trace record whose selection caused it, and the tick already holds the previous trace record; comparing them answers `did the previous write land?` from evidence. The two sides now share ONE identity rule (trace-record-identity, war_machine.clj:1610-1618, which clock-source at war_machine.clj:1977-1984 delegates to) so writer and reader cannot drift and report a landed write as missing. The read had been DROPPING witness.source since S4; active-clock-edge->mission keeps it under this row's flag only (war_machine.clj:1525-1548). IMPLEMENTATION, BEHIND A DECLARED INPUT, DEFAULT OFF: FUTON_WM_FOCUS_RECONCILE / *focus-reconcile?* (war_machine.clj:125). Reconciler focus-reconciliation (war_machine.clj:1669-1738), attached by tick-mission-focus (war_machine.clj:1740-1783) and called once at war_machine.clj:6195-6196 with prev-trace-record. :previous-write-landed? is THREE-VALUED (true / false / :unknown with a named :unknown-reason), because `not landed` and `not measurable` are different findings. U21's :agrees-with-durable? IS KEPT AND NOT OVERWRITTEN -- it is pinned by tests and by a committed replay, so the typed :case is added beside it; flag off, tick-mission-focus returns U21's map and the S4 read keeps its exact four-key shape, pinned at war_machine_test.clj:2022-2049. NON-CONSUMPTION IS STRUCTURAL: the reconciliation is computed from wm-decision and prev-trace-record, both final where they are bound, and attached after ranking and selection, so no rank, weight, temperature, admissibility verdict or selector can read it. HALF (b), THE RENDER: render-active-mission-line (war_machine.clj:4058-4066) is UNCHANGED and still renders the witnessed edge; render-mission-focus-line (war_machine.clj:4068-4109) is a new SECOND line appended after it at war_machine.clj:4126-4128, never folded into it, and it reads no environment variable -- it renders what the judgement carries, so the case clause appears only when the record itself carries a :reconciliation. Byte-identical rendering of the durable line and the two-line non-collapse are both asserted (war_machine_test.clj:2196-2247). THE REPLAY: holes/labs/wm-contract/u43_focus_reconcile.clj over the three 2026-09-02 trace records; artifacts runs/U43-focus-reconcile/U43-FOCUS-RECONCILE.txt and cases.edn. Replay only -- no live tick, no run lock, nothing written under data/. All EIGHT cases produced what they declared and all eight are in the declared vocabulary; the script exits 1 if either control fails, and it did fail on its first run, which is how a bad fixture was caught (the corpus pair it had picked came from two different records and agreed, so three cases silently collapsed to :agrees). Two consecutive runs are byte-identical. WHAT THE LIVE CORPUS ACTUALLY READS, and it is a finding rather than a gap: NO record reaches :lagged-write-landed or :lagged-write-missing, because the S4 read dropped witness.source until this row and so no persisted :active-mission carries the discriminating field. 0a18c4f7 reads :no-durable-edge; 4abad68c and 801976e7 read :lag-unattributable -- the divergence is real and its cause is not yet measurable. It becomes measurable on the first tick run with the flag on; the two landed/missing cases in the artifact are marked :constructed and their construction is ONE field, the witness source. U21's boolean returns false on those three AND on :clock-not-read and :durable-unreadable, which is the row's finding in one number. NOT REPAIRED, AND SAID SO: record-selection-clock! is still fire-and-forget; a tick that records :lagged-write-missing does not re-attempt the write. Making the dropped write VISIBLE is what this row does; repairing it needs its own witness rule named and is out of scope. Not minted as a row -- recorded in the artifact section 6 and in the registry :not-claimed. REGISTRY: :choices :focus-clock-reconciliation, :status :observed-not-decided, per TN section 1 point 3 -- an observation of what the machine does behind a default-off flag, not a ruling; nothing added to control-map-edges.edn :decisions. GATES: clj-kondo 0 errors 0 warnings on all three touched files; check-parens OK; negative_controls.sh PASS (22 negative, 12 positive); pointer_check.bb 1019 pointers in 3 files, 0 unresolved; worklist_check 129 items OK with 19 signed registry entries verified unchanged. TESTS: 1160 tests / 10437 assertions / 0 failures / 0 errors over all 87 futon2.* namespaces (clojure -X:test :nses [...]), of which futon2.report.war-machine-test is 79 tests / 464