ad of a certificate. The accepted-run authority is :pin/accepted-steps in data/wm-step/w1/pin/pin.edn, the list wm_step_observe.bb:125 reads, and it names exactly two runs, 2026-09-04-009-det-b and 2026-09-04-010-accepted. Neither is the R5 pilot, which is why the checker prints 0 converged. So the ruling is discharged in its BUILD (three legs, both checkers refusing) and not in its TERMINATING CONDITION (zero converged), and which of the two this row is measured by is the reviewer's call. Closing the gap is a RUN and not a slice of this row: it needs the pre-flight and data/wm-trace/.run-lock, and which run qualifies for acceptance is the call the RUN4 ruling already reserves to Joe at certificate time. DECISION 2 -- HOW THE THIRTEEN :covers-key ENTRIES ARE SIGNED. The row carries thirteen entries, no :review-covers and no :reviewed-by -- read off the row, not assumed. worklist_check.bb:132-135 collects signed rows only when :status is :done, and worklist_check.bb:167-168 refuses a :done row that has :covers-key without :review-covers. So the check is silent today and fires the moment this row closes, and it will then compare all thirteen entries against the single sha the signature names. The decision is whether one signature at one sha covers thirteen entries moved by thirteen different slices -- whether the reviewer has read all thirteen -- not what the mechanism does with the sha. :blocked is a safe holding state for exactly that reason: it is not :done, so no signature is demanded and none is invented to satisfy a check. NOTHING HERE IS A RULING: aif-equations.edn :choices is byte-unchanged, no control-map-edges.edn :decisions entry was written, no source under src/ changed, gen_aif_dag.bb was not run, no tick was taken and the run lock was not held. No registry entry moved, so no signature was staled and no supersession was needed."
