  :evidence "SLICE DONE, ROW STILL OPEN (leg 3 slice 2, THE REFUSING CHECKER OVER THE CONVERGENCE LEDGER; the accounts of leg 1, of the :code sweep, of leg 2 and of leg 3 slice 1 are C517-C531 and are not repeated here). THE ACCOUNT IS C532-F8-convergence-checker.md, whose last section is the review. convergence_check.bb recomputes rather than reads: the eighteen row ids against the equation registry both directions, all 54 identity fields byte-equal, all 36 licences resolved by suffix with an ambiguous path REFUSED rather than taken as a first hit, the eight-rung order, every leading leg from its two rungs, both certificate forms, and accepted-run status derived from the pin. Nineteen negative controls, one acceptance control and one positive control are section 14 of the shared suite. THE RUN-IDENTITY DECISION WAS THE IMPLEMENTER'S TO MAKE AND IT MADE IT: a witnessed-or-higher rung is licensed by a run identity found anywhere in the artifact AND a quantity-specific record predicate, which admits the F7 receipt whose :run-id is deliberately nested at :basis :run-id while refusing a fixture that merely carries some nested id; the F6 top-level count is printed separately and is zero. THE PACKET WAS WRONG BEFORE THE DELIVERY WAS, and the first dispatch is what found it: requirement (h) was transcribed key-shaped, demanding an explicit :trailing-leg reason that the three artifact certificates do not carry, while the acceptance bar demanded the committed ledger pass. codex-9 wrote nothing, reported the contradiction and refused to edit the ledger to fit -- which is the packet's own instruction working, since a checker that repairs its subject until the subject passes is not a check. This seat adjudicated the two forms (artifact: :artifact + :green? + :accepted-run? + :reason, three rows, all naming the R5 pilot, green and not accepted, with :reason as where they say why; trailing-leg: :trailing-leg + :reason + :what-would-license, fifteen rows) and re-dispatched. Adding